{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Iron Snow: Rusticles on the Titanic's Hull",
    "date": "2026-07-28",
    "lesson": "Deep shipwrecks in the North Atlantic become artificial iron seeps. Halomonas titanicae and related iron-oxidizing bacteria colonize steel hulls, forming 'rusticles' - porous, icicle-like mineral structures that consume iron for energy and slowly dissolve the wreck. This is chemolithotrophy: energy comes from oxidizing Fe(0)/Fe(2+) to Fe(3+) oxides, not from sunlight or organic food. The rusticle itself is a habitat - a porous mineral lattice riddled with channels where the bacteria shelter, much like the wreck it is eating. Deep, near-freezing, lightless water and immense pressure make this a slow, patient, cold-metabolism world where growth is measured in decades, not days.",
    "citation": "Sanchez-Porro et al. 2010, Halomonas titanicae sp. nov.; Cullimore & Johnston rusticle studies, RMS Titanic wreck site, 3800m depth",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "RMS Titanic wreck, North Atlantic, 3800m depth - Halomonas titanicae iron-oxidizing rusticle colonies"
  },
